@database "agent_watchdog"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: agent_watchdog.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Agent Watchdog"
@{h1}Agent Watchdog

A long agent run can go in circles -- the same search again and again, a
file read three times, a question reopened without anything new -- and
from the outside it looks just like a run that is working hard. Agent
Watchdog watches an agent that switched it on: every few steps a second,
cheap model reads a short excerpt of the run and answers one question --
did the recent stretch make a decision or close an open question? Its
verdict (@{tt}continue@{utt}, @{tt}steer@{utt} or @{tt}abort@{utt}) is
written to a log and shown as a status line in the chat.

@{b}It only watches.@{ub} This is the first stage of the plugin: nothing is
told to the agent, nothing is stopped, the agent's history is not touched.
The log is there to find out whether the verdicts are good enough to act
on. It has no tools and no panel.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
  @{"  The verdict log  " link log}

For agents and developers
  @{"  The hooks  " link hooks}
  @{"  The judge  " link judge}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The entry in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}, as shipped:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    agent_watchdog:
      type: agent_watchdog
      enabled: true
      config:
        llm_profile: "or-deepseek-flash"
@{body}

The plugin is on, but all three of its hooks are off for every agent. No
allowlist line -- there are no tools. No extra packages.

@{h2}Switching it on for an agent

In the agent's YAML. Checks every few steps need one hook:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  hooks:
    overrides:
      agent_watchdog.observe_step:
        enabled: true
        first_check_step: 10
        every_n_steps: 10
@{body}

Checks inside one long model call (a model that thinks for minutes before
it acts) need two more, always together -- see
@{" The hooks " link hooks}:

@{code yaml}
      agent_watchdog.remember_task:
        enabled: true
      agent_watchdog.observe_reasoning:
        enabled: true
        every_n_reasoning_chars: 20000
@{body}

Each agent decides for itself: a sub-agent is watched only when its own
YAML switches the hooks on, not because its parent does. Chat sessions,
sub-agents and woken runs of an agent that has the hooks on are all
watched.

@{h2}Settings

Every key except @{tt}llm_profile@{utt} and @{tt}log_path@{utt} can be set
in two places: under @{tt}config:@{utt} in the server entry (for every
agent), and in an agent's override of a hook (for that agent and that
hook). The agent's value wins.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
llm_profile | turbo | the judge's LLM profile; server entry only
judge_prompt | empty | a prompt file for the judge, relative to the project root; empty means the built-in one
first_check_step | 10 | the step of the first check
every_n_steps | 10 | steps between checks after the first
every_n_reasoning_chars | 20000 | characters of thinking in one call between checks inside it
task_chars | 4000 | characters of user messages the judge sees, newest first
spec_chars | 3000 | characters from the end of the system prompt
reasoning_chars | 8000 | characters of the most recent thinking
max_tool_calls | 20 | how many of the latest tool calls the judge sees
judge_timeout_seconds | 120 | a judge slower than this counts as continue
log_path | empty | the log file, relative to the project root; empty means @{tt}agent_watchdog/verdicts.jsonl@{utt} in the data directory
@{body}

Steps are counted from 0 within one run, the way the agent loop counts
them: @{tt}first_check_step: 10@{utt} is the eleventh model call, shown
as step 11 in the chat. Every new message to the agent starts a new run
at step 0, so a conversation of short turns is never checked by
@{tt}observe_step@{utt}.

A number setting that is not a whole number of at least 1 (0, a word,
@{tt}true@{utt}) is logged as a warning and the server entry's value is
used -- or the default above, when that one is not valid either.

@{h2}Which hook reads which key

An agent's override belongs to one hook, and each hook reads only its own:

@{table}
Hook | Reads
observe_step | first_check_step, every_n_steps, task_chars, spec_chars, reasoning_chars, max_tool_calls, judge_prompt, judge_timeout_seconds
remember_task | task_chars, spec_chars, max_tool_calls
observe_reasoning | every_n_reasoning_chars, reasoning_chars, judge_prompt, judge_timeout_seconds
@{body}

So an agent that wants a custom judge prompt for both kinds of check sets
@{tt}judge_prompt@{utt} in the @{tt}observe_step@{utt} override and in
the @{tt}observe_reasoning@{utt} override.

@{h2}Where the files are

@{bullet}The log: @{tt}agent_watchdog/verdicts.jsonl@{utt} in the data
directory (@{tt}data/@{utt} of the installation unless another one is
configured). One file for every agent and every user. See
@{" The verdict log " link log}.
@{bullet}The built-in judge prompt: @{tt}prompts/judge.md@{utt} in the
plugin folder, read once when the plugin starts. A configured
@{tt}judge_prompt@{utt} file is read at its first check and then kept.
Editing either takes a restart -- on purpose, so a save in the middle of a
run cannot change how that run is judged.
@endnode

@node hooks "The hooks"
@{h1}The hooks

All three are off by default; an agent switches them on as
@{" Setting it up " link setup} shows. None of them changes anything the
agent sees, and none of them holds the agent up: the judge runs in the
background, the hook itself returns at once.

@{h2}observe_step

A @{tt}post_llm_call@{utt} hook: it runs after every model call of the
agent. At a due step -- @{tt}first_check_step@{utt}, then every
@{tt}every_n_steps@{utt} -- it builds the excerpt (see
@{" The judge " link judge}) and starts the judge.

It does nothing when the step is not due, and when the previous check of
the same run is still waiting for its answer: that step is skipped, not
queued, and the next due step tries again.

@{h2}remember_task and observe_reasoning

A model that thinks for a long time inside one call has no step boundary
to check at. @{tt}observe_reasoning@{utt} is an @{tt}llm_progress@{utt}
hook: it runs while the call is still streaming, every 2000 characters of
thinking, and starts the judge each time the call's thinking crosses
another @{tt}every_n_reasoning_chars@{utt}. The judge then sees the call's
own thinking so far, marked as still in progress.

Inside the stream the hook has no access to the conversation, so
@{tt}remember_task@{utt}, a @{tt}pre_llm_call@{utt} hook, builds the
excerpt before every call and keeps it -- only the excerpt, for the last
32 runs of the process. Without it, every check inside a call is logged as
@{tt}task_not_visible@{utt} and no judge is asked.

@{tt}observe_reasoning@{utt} is blind on clients that stream no thinking
(Gemini, batch mode): there is nothing to count. A retry of the call
starts counting at 0 again.

@{h2}The status line

While the judge works, the chat shows a line of its own below the
agent's -- @{tt}checking step 10@{utt} -- and when it is done, the
verdict:

@{code text}
continue: the agent decided to read the config loader next — coder
continue (evidence_not_verbatim): no new information since the last search — coder
continue (timeout) — step 20 of coder
@{body}

A @{tt}steer@{utt} or @{tt}abort@{utt} is not an error and is not shown
as one. The line is cut at 140 characters.

@{h2}At shutdown

When the process ends, a judge that is still running is waited for, up to
@{tt}judge_timeout_seconds@{utt} from the server entry. One still running
after that is stopped and logged as @{tt}cancelled_at_shutdown@{utt}. A
short-lived agent-cli run therefore still gets the line for its last
check.
@endnode

@node judge "The judge"
@{h1}The judge

A separate model, from the profile in @{tt}llm_profile@{utt}. It gets the
judge prompt as its system message and the excerpt as one user message,
and has no tools. One client is made at the first check and used for every
agent of the process.

@{h2}The excerpt

Bounded, however long the run is:

@{table}
Field | What it holds
user_messages | the user messages of the run, newest first into @{tt}task_chars@{utt}, shown oldest first. Notes the run itself added (the step budget, "provide your final answer now") count as user messages
expected_result | the last @{tt}spec_chars@{utt} of the system prompt, where a deliverable is usually described
recent_thinking | the most recent @{tt}reasoning_chars@{utt} of the agent's thinking (or text, when a step has no thinking)
recent_tool_calls | the latest @{tt}max_tool_calls@{utt} calls: tool name, a fingerprint of the arguments, the result as ok, empty, error or pending, and its size
call_in_progress | true for a check inside a call
@{body}

Tool arguments are never shown -- for a writing tool they are the whole
file. Identical fingerprints mean identical arguments. A result counts as
@{tt}empty@{utt} when it is empty, or JSON whose top-level lists are all
empty (a search that "succeeded" with no hits); as @{tt}error@{utt} when
it is JSON with @{tt}status: error@{utt} or an @{tt}error@{utt} field.

A run with no user message at all is not judged: the check is logged as
@{tt}task_not_visible@{utt}.

@{h2}The answer

The judge answers with a JSON object: @{tt}verdict@{utt},
@{tt}reason@{utt}, @{tt}evidence@{utt} (a word-for-word quote from the
excerpt) and, for steer and abort, @{tt}message@{utt} -- what the agent
would be told. The built-in prompt tells it: when in doubt, continue.

Everything doubtful counts as @{tt}continue@{utt}, and the log line says
why in @{tt}fail_open@{utt}:

@{table}
fail_open | When
unparseable | the answer is not JSON (a code fence around it is fine)
not_an_object | JSON, but not an object
unknown_verdict | the verdict is none of the three
no_evidence | no quote
evidence_not_verbatim | the quote is not in the excerpt (spaces, case and a leading or trailing "..." do not matter)
no_message | steer or abort without a message
timeout | no answer within @{tt}judge_timeout_seconds@{utt}
judge_error | the call failed -- network, provider error, refusal, unknown profile; the text is in @{tt}error@{utt}
no_judge_llm | the agent carries no system configuration to build the judge from
cancelled_at_shutdown | still running when the process ended
task_not_visible | no user message to show, or a check inside a call without remember_task; no call was made
@{body}

A judge that is broken therefore never looks like a judge that approves of
everything: count the lines with @{tt}fail_open@{utt}.

@{h2}A prompt of your own

@{tt}judge_prompt@{utt} names a Markdown file, relative to the project
root. It has to keep the output contract -- the JSON fields and the
verbatim quote -- or every check is logged as fail-open. A file that
cannot be read falls back to the built-in prompt; the log line then
carries @{tt}judge_prompt@{utt} and @{tt}judge_prompt_error@{utt}.
@endnode

@node log "The verdict log"
@{h1}The verdict log

One JSON line per check, appended to @{tt}verdicts.jsonl@{utt} (see
@{" Setting it up " link setup} for where it is). Nothing ever trims it.

@{table}
Field | Meaning
ts | when the line was written, UTC
request_id, session_id, agent | the run that was watched
observed_model | the model the agent was running on
step | the agent's step, counted from 0
trigger | step or reasoning
reasoning_chars | for a check inside a call: its thinking so far
task_preview | the first 200 characters of the newest user message the judge saw
judge_prompt, judge_prompt_error | when a prompt file is configured
judge_model | the judge's model
verdict, reason, evidence, message | the judge's answer as used
usage, finish_reason | the judge call's token usage and stop reason
latency_ms | how long the judge took
fail_open | why the answer was not used as given; see @{" The judge " link judge}
raw | when the answer itself was not usable (unparseable to no_message): its first 2000 characters
error | with judge_error: what failed
@{body}

The judge's calls do not go through the watched agent's LLM hooks: they
are not in the message debugger and do not count toward the agent's
usage. @{tt}usage@{utt} in this log is the only record of what the judge
costs.

The log holds the beginning of every watched user message, for every user
of the installation, in one file.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{b}The watched agent sees nothing.@{ub} No message is added, no message is
changed, every hook returns without modifying the call. The
@{tt}message@{utt} a steer or abort verdict carries goes into the log
only.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

None for the agent: its history and its cached prompt are untouched, and
the agent does not wait for the judge. The judge costs one call of its own
model per check. The excerpt is bounded by the settings -- by default at
most about 15 000 characters plus the tool call list and the prompt
(about 3 300 characters) -- no matter how long the run is. With the
defaults a run of 50 model calls is checked four times (steps 10,
20, 30 and 40); a check inside a call adds
one per 20 000 characters of thinking.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}No intervention: the verdicts are logged, not acted on.
@{bullet}@{tt}expected_result@{utt} is the end of the system prompt, not a
located "deliverable" section; a prompt that describes its output
elsewhere gives the judge the wrong part.
@{bullet}Refusals are not counted on their own: they are
@{tt}judge_error@{utt} lines with the provider's text.
@{bullet}A step that falls due while the previous check still runs is
skipped, not caught up.
@{bullet}Beyond 32 runs remembering their task at the same time, the oldest
one's checks inside a call become @{tt}task_not_visible@{utt}.
@{bullet}The log is not split by user and never trimmed.
@{bullet}Shutdown waits the server entry's
@{tt}judge_timeout_seconds@{utt}, even when an agent set a longer one.
@endnode
